    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d144">

                <pb facs="0211" n="195" sameAs="frus:frus1951v07p1/pg195"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">795.00/2–2351: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Gifford</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1951-02-23T19:00:00+00:00">February 23,
                            1951—7 p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>4595. Australian High Commissioner has informed Foreign Office of receipt of
                    message from Australian Ambassador Washington reporting conversation with
                    Secretary Rusk in which latter was quoted as stating he foresees two
                    alternatives for Korea: (<hi rendition="#italic">a</hi>) Unification by
                    agreement which would probably lead to eventual seizure of power by Commies
                    throughout Korea or (<hi rendition="#italic">b</hi>) Reversion to <hi
                        rendition="#italic">status quo ante</hi> June 25. Of these two alternatives,
                    Rusk said to have indicated preference for (<hi rendition="#italic">b</hi>).</p>
                <p>In Foreign Office thinking alternative (<hi rendition="#italic">a</hi>) would be
                    preferable as honorable settlement if it could be acccomplished without military
                    defeat. In long term, UK feels effect in Korea would be the same, as reversion
                    to <hi rendition="#italic">status quo</hi> would merely reopen ROK to Communist penetration. Foreign Office
                    inquires informally whether views expressed by Rusk, as reported by Australian
                    Ambassador, represent official US views or, as more likely, Rusk’s informal
                    thinking.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gifford</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
